#{[17592186139556 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186139557 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139556 #edb/ref 1061 8720 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139556 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "11ec6bb7-b9b3-51ea-b1b2-5f6a0a7b75b9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139556 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186139635 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139557 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186139558 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139557 #edb/ref 1061 8722 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139557 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "fa4d60ee-75e2-54e2-b7eb-e1946cf89388" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139557 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186139636 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139558 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186139559 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139558 #edb/ref 1061 8723 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139558 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ab8e5676-8695-5fd6-8d78-6bae4eba68f9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139558 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186138558 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139559 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186139560 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139559 #edb/ref 1061 8724 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139559 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "df7668d0-a581-5a0f-a689-b2079711a0d2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139559 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186138559 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139560 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186139561 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139560 #edb/ref 1061 8725 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139560 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "24ee0c7c-ff8c-5b75-b742-a7f922b432ab" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139560 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186138560 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139561 #edb/ref 1061 8726 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139561 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e6dafd2a-98a4-508e-8294-b9f6ffd16d06" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139561 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186138561 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139635 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's look at the three points $A,$ $B,$ and $C$ below. \n\n![](images/95/956f9b2c979f06897b09119b089ced903e095d72136e64b9ea52e946fba41544.png)\n\nAs indicated in the diagram, the distances between the points are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB=4\\,cm,\\,BC=2\\,cm,\\,AC=6\\,cm.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCan we be sure that the points are collinear? To answer this, we use the **segment addition postulate**.\n\nThe postulate states the following: \n\n*Three points are collinear if and only if the largest distance between two of them is equal to the sum of the other two distances.*\n\nHere, the largest distance is $AC,$ so the points $A,$ $B,$ and $C$ are collinear *if and only if*\n\n$$\nAC=AB + BC.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given information $AC=6\\,\\mathrm{cm},$ $AB=4\\,\\mathrm{cm},$ and $BC=2\\,\\mathrm{cm}$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AC & =AB+BC \\\\ 6 & =4+2 \\\\ 6 & =6.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a true statement, so the three points $A,$ $B,$ and $C$ are indeed collinear.\n\n**Note:** The postulate works in both directions:\n\n- If the points $A,$ $B,$ and $C$ are collinear then $AC= AB + BC.$\n\n- If $AC= AB + BC$ then the points $A,$ $B,$ and $C$ are collinear." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139635 #edb/ref 1065 2168 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139635 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "0a460529-da55-5fcc-a207-e4ae6a47f12d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139635 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139636 #edb/ref 1064 "Given two of the three distances between three collinear points, we can calculate the third distance using the segment addition postulate. \n\nTo demonstrate, consider the collinear points $A,$ $B,$ and $C$ below.\n\n![](images/3b/3b1bc383e8d570f9dc33346e6f4ae04377e3c5c4c0d0e53abe07b0aacd9bb3e6.png)\n\nWe are given two of the distances:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB=3\\,cm,\\,AC=5\\,cm\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the third distance, $BC,$ using the segment addition postulate.\n\nFirst, we state the equation and substitute the known quantities $AB=3$ and $AC=5,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AC & =AB+BC \\\\ 5 & =3+BC\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for $BC$ by subtracting $3$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5 & =3+BC \\\\ BC & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance between the points $B$ and $C$ is $2\\,\\textrm{cm}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139636 #edb/ref 1065 2169 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139636 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "92286c39-6af9-5ff0-b2e9-fa44fec8f732" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139636 #edb/ref 1067 "Calculating the Distance Between Collinear Points" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139661 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose that we want to simplify the following expression: \n\n$$\n2+\\dfrac{5}{2}x -5 + \\dfrac{7}{2}x.\n$$\n\n  \n\nWe begin by collecting like terms.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\frac{5}{2}x+\\frac{7}{2}x)+(2-5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we combine the like terms, simplifying the coefficients as much as possible.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\frac{5}{2}x+\\frac{7}{2}x)+(2-5) & = \\\\ (\\frac{5+7}{2})x+(-3) & = \\\\ \\frac{12}{2}x-3 & = \\\\ 6x-3 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the simplified expression is $6x-3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139661 #edb/ref 1065 8040 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139661 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "7ef8a0d2-a9fe-57f8-b3fc-7b69cbb3a818" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139661 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184225 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049923 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184225 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186184801 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184225 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186184802 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184225 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186503673 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184225 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186503703 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184225 #edb/ref 1039 "Combining Radical Expressions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184225 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186184131 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184225 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "37603549-5c55-5b9e-8d2d-2121db167d02" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184320 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050890 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184320 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186184740 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184320 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186184803 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184320 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186503733 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184320 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186503743 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184320 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186563692 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184320 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186563731 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184320 #edb/ref 1039 "Combining Radical Expressions Using the Rules of Absolute Value" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184320 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186183935 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184320 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "5fcba20f-b43b-558d-bd84-d76816d3186b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184321 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048170 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184321 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186184500 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184321 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186184501 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184321 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186503701 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184321 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186503731 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184321 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186563675 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184321 #edb/ref 1039 "Simplifying a Cube Root Expression" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184321 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186183936 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184321 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "de2b7926-fa13-5636-9682-8fa382336be7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184432 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186185015 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184432 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186185016 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184432 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186185195 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184432 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186185196 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184432 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186185197 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184432 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184432 #edb/ref 1045 "Combining Radical Expressions Using the Product Rule" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184432 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186185016 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184432 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048132 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184432 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184432 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "a963d35a-d51d-50f7-b102-8821b5ecb011" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185015 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186185195 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185015 #edb/ref 1061 37583 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185015 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2a88297e-0c93-59fc-a45e-bb8dc09591fe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185015 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186184225 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185016 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186185015 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185016 #edb/ref 1061 37588 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185016 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9a6b95b2-a3b2-5264-9183-0fefe3abfed8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185016 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186185346 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185195 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186185196 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185195 #edb/ref 1061 4307 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185195 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "83175ccc-7da0-50d9-b695-82571c0cb353" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185195 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186184320 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185196 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186185197 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185196 #edb/ref 1061 4313 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185196 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c0a3a07c-d5b4-5d4b-805c-3c93987ee6fe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185196 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186185310 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185197 #edb/ref 1061 4314 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185197 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d028b29f-988d-5038-a9c2-8a95b472232b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185197 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186184321 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185310 #edb/ref 1064 "The product rule for radicals also works for higher-order roots, like the cube root. In the case of the cube root, we have\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[3]{ab} = \\sqrt[3]{a}\\cdot \\sqrt[3]{b}.\n$$\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[n]{ab} = \\sqrt[n]{a}\\cdot \\sqrt[n]{b}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185310 #edb/ref 1065 1607 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185310 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "f9406c08-e6de-514e-aeae-9f581d5730a5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185310 #edb/ref 1067 "The Product Rule for Cube Roots" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185346 #edb/ref 1064 "The **product rule for radicals** states that the square root of a product equals the product of the square roots:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\sqrt{a} \\cdot \\sqrt{b} = \\sqrt{ab}\n\t}\n$$\n\nWe can use the product rule to combine radical expressions. \n\nFor example, let's suppose we want to combine the radicals in the following expression:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{5x} \\cdot \\sqrt{6z}\n$$\n\nTo combine the radicals, we apply the product rule as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5x}\\cdot \\sqrt{6z} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{5x\\cdot 6z} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{30xz} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185346 #edb/ref 1065 6200 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185346 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "1c9d8f0d-315b-5c83-a8e6-17638c1c3d24" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185346 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188243 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049747 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188243 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050702 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188243 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050843 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188243 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186188886 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188243 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186188958 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188243 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186510366 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188243 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186510403 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188243 #edb/ref 1039 "Converting From Kilograms to Grams" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188243 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186188179 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188243 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "12bd9bab-79dd-577e-907e-5f537b3b5eac" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188244 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186188887 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188244 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186188921 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188244 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186510405 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188244 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186510407 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188244 #edb/ref 1039 "Converting From Grams to Milligrams" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188244 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186188180 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188244 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "278e33dd-6216-5cf9-9e52-e20924e0622b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188245 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049142 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188245 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050749 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188245 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186188876 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188245 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186188877 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188245 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186510448 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188245 #edb/ref 1039 "Converting From Smaller Units to Larger Units" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188245 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186188182 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188245 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "0018c984-790b-57d0-b086-77f5bdf8f273" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188462 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186189032 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188462 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186189033 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188462 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186189034 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188462 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186189035 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188462 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186189036 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188462 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188462 #edb/ref 1045 "Unit Conversions Using Base Units of Mass" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188462 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186189032 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188462 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050282 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188462 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188462 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "991198d2-0d13-559b-aa50-a822d61cae0c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189032 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186189033 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189032 #edb/ref 1061 142504 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189032 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a5a6a7de-dd8f-533d-8cb9-defc2774fef3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189032 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186189493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189033 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186189034 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189033 #edb/ref 1061 142505 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189033 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3b9c48be-9be5-5ad9-8524-1db31336c531" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189033 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186188243 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189034 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186189035 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189034 #edb/ref 1061 142509 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189034 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d06d61a6-e2a7-5788-ac3a-dc0591173d8d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189034 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186188244 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189035 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186189036 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189035 #edb/ref 1061 142513 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189035 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a44cd1ff-e34d-5106-a89c-28301fc8fca6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189035 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186189495 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189036 #edb/ref 1061 142514 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189036 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2178685b-38fc-5e05-805e-7615b35b6f75" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189036 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186188245 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189493 #edb/ref 1064 "We can manipulate units the same way as variables, which often provides a convenient way to solve problems. One type of problem where units can help is converting between different units of measurement. \n\nFor example, let's use units to convert $0.03\\,\\textrm{kg}$ into grams. We start by recalling the conversion table for mass units. \n\n| Name | Symbol | Conversion |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Kilogram | $\\textrm{kg}$ | $1\\,\\textrm{kg} = 1\\,000\\,\\textrm{g}$ |\n| Gram | $\\textrm{g}$ | Base Unit |\n| Milligram | $\\textrm{mg}$ | $1\\,000\\,\\textrm{mg} = 1\\,\\textrm{g}$ |\n\nSince we're converting from kilograms to grams, we need a conversion between these two units. According to our table, there are $1000\\,\\textrm g$ per $\\textrm{kg}.$ We can write this ratio as a fraction, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1000\\,\\textrm g}{\\textrm{kg}}\n$$\n\nThis is our **conversion factor.** It's important to write the conversion factor with the units included.\n\nTo convert $0.03 \\,\\textrm{kg}$ to grams, we multiply this quantity by our conversion factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.03\\,\\text{kg} & =0.03\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot \\frac{1\\,000\\,\\text{g}}{\\text{kg}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the kilogram units $(\\textrm{kg})$ cancel, leaving only gram units $(\\textrm{g}).$ Consequently, our expression can be simplified as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.03\\,\\text{kg} & =0.03\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot \\frac{1\\,000\\,\\text{g}}{\\text{kg}} \\\\  & =0.03\\,\\cancel{\\text{kg}}\\cdot \\frac{1\\,000\\,\\text{g}}{\\cancel{\\text{kg}}} \\\\  & =0.03\\cdot 1\\,000\\,\\text{g} \\\\  & =30\\,\\text{g}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $0.03 \\,\\textrm{kg}$ is equivalent to $30 \\,\\text{g}.$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- When multiplying by the conversion factor, the units we want to remove should *always* cancel, and the units we wish to keep should *always* remain. If this isn't the case, we've made a mistake somewhere.\n\n- We can think of the conversion factor as equivalent to the number $1,$ and multiplying a quantity by $1$ doesn't change it. Therefore, multiplying $0.03\\,\\textrm{kg}$ by the conversion factor doesn't change the *size* of the quantity, though it does change the units.\n\nFinally, notice that, in this example, we've converted from a \"larger\" unit of mass $(\\textrm{kg})$ to a smaller one $(\\textrm{g}).$ We'll see how to use units to convert from smaller units of mass to larger units later in this lesson." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189493 #edb/ref 1065 8735 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189493 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "a61c3c5b-cabe-50e6-bd8c-c451fc7f7329" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189493 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189495 #edb/ref 1064 "We've seen how to use units to convert from larger units of mass to smaller ones. Let's now learn how to convert from smaller units to larger ones.\n\nFor example, suppose we want to convert $600\\,\\textrm{mg}$ to grams. We start by recalling the conversion table for mass units. \n\n| Name | Symbol | Conversion |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Kilogram | $\\textrm{kg}$ | $1\\,\\textrm{kg} = 1\\,000\\,\\textrm{g}$ |\n| Gram | $\\textrm{g}$ | Base Unit |\n| Milligram | $\\textrm{mg}$ | $1\\,000\\,\\textrm{mg} = 1\\,\\textrm{g}$ |\n\nThere are $1000\\,\\textrm {mg}$ per $\\textrm{g},$ which we can write as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1000\\,\\textrm{mg}}{\\textrm{g}}\n$$\n\nHowever, since we're converting from a smaller unit of mass $(\\textrm{mg})$ to a larger one $(\\textrm{g}),$ we construct our conversion factor by taking the *reciprocal* of the above:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{g}}{1000\\,\\textrm{mg}}\n$$\n\nNow, multiplying $600 \\,\\textrm{mg}$ by our conversion factor, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}600\\,\\text{mg} & =600\\,\\text{mg}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{g}}{1\\,000\\,\\text{mg}} \\\\  & =600\\,\\cancel{\\text{mg}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{g}}{1\\,000\\,\\cancel{\\text{mg}}} \\\\  & =600\\cdot \\frac{\\text{g}}{1\\,000} \\\\  & =\\frac{600}{1000}\\,\\text{g} \\\\  & =0.6\\,\\text{g}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $600 \\,\\textrm{mg}$ is equivalent to $0.6\\,\\text{g}.$\n\nNote the following: \n\n- When we say that grams are a \"larger unit of mass\" than milligrams, this simply means that $1\\,\\textrm g$ is a larger mass than $1\\,\\textrm{mg}.$\n\n- If you forget to take the reciprocal to find the conversion factor, the units will not cancel when you multiply. This indicates that a mistake has occurred. In such cases, it's best to double-check that you're using the correct conversion factor." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189495 #edb/ref 1065 8739 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189495 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "295b5ac0-e435-50fd-9493-4e753d8c31bb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189495 #edb/ref 1067 "Using Reciprocals to Convert From Smaller Units to Larger Units" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351989 #edb/ref 1080 "800" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351989 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351989 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2db54839-6a5b-5b75-9ac0-2fe3487ce8c1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186352191 #edb/ref 1080 "100" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186352191 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186352191 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "43e30190-4e81-5fda-b3a5-079b052411c9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186352192 #edb/ref 1080 "40" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186352192 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186352192 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "43f1421d-10d0-5af6-8262-60e55e5615e1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186352294 #edb/ref 1080 "200" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186352294 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186352294 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "50c4006c-d6f4-5cb3-977c-4236359a9cc9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186352666 #edb/ref 1080 "400" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186352666 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186352666 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "7d349513-c9f4-5a55-81fa-6b46f8d0946e" #edb/